{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "FastJX",
    "description": "Fast-JX photolysis rates, upstream `FastJX(t_ref)` with its default `fluxes = :scattering`: the direct solar beam is computed online by spherical Beer-Lambert attenuation through a fixed climatological column (Rayleigh + O2 + O3) and upstream's precomputed clear-sky diffuse field is added, both scaled by the Earth-Sun distance factor. 0-D and stateless: every output is an observed function of (t + t_ref, lat, long, T, P, H2O). Exposes the 13 J-rates of upstream's `:superfast` set (j_H2O2, j_H2COa, j_H2COb, j_O31D, j_o32OH, j_CH3OOH, j_NO2, j_ActAld, j_PAN, j_NO3a, j_NO3b, j_N2O5, j_O3) and the 18 band fluxes F_<nm>.",
    "authors": ["GasChem.jl / GasChemMTK.jl contributors", "EarthSciModels"],
    "created": "2026-10-28T00:00:00Z",
    "tags": [
      "chemistry",
      "photolysis",
      "fast-jx",
      "scattering",
      "spherical-geometry",
      "interp-linear",
      "interp-bilinear"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Wild, O., Zhu, X., and Prather, M. J. (2000), Fast-J: Accurate simulation of in- and below-cloud photolysis in tropospheric chemical models, J. Atmos. Chem., 37, 245-282."
      },
      {
        "citation": "Neu, J. L., Prather, M. J., and Penner, J. E. (2007), Global atmospheric chemistry: Integrating over fractional cloud cover, J. Geophys. Res., 112, D11306, doi:10.1029/2006JD008007."
      },
      {
        "citation": "Cross sections and quantum yields on the 18-bin grid of GEOS-Chem's CHEM_INPUTS/CLOUD_J/v2024-09 FJX_spec.dat (bins 17-18 at 380/574 nm); clear-sky diffuse field from a Julia port of the Cloud-J Feautrier solver (geoschem/cloud-j@16d18b07)."
      },
      {
        "citation": "Source: jialinl6/GasChemMTK.jl@37792b29 (branch superfast-gas-only), src/Fast-JX.jl, src/direct_flux.jl, src/interpolations_FastJX.jl, src/tropospheric_interpolation_data.bson, src/diffuse_flux_data.bson."
      }
    ]
  },
  "models": {
    "FastJX": {
      "reference": {
        "notes": "source_ref: jialinl6/GasChemMTK.jl@37792b29 (branch superfast-gas-only):src/Fast-JX.jl FastJX (fluxes = :scattering), src/direct_flux.jl, src/interpolations_FastJX.jl flux_sys_scattering.\n\nDirect beam. Upstream's calc_direct_flux(cosSZA, P, k) snaps P to an edge of the GEOS 72-layer grid (find_closest_pressure_index), builds the 147-point fine-grid air-mass factors with sphere2J and applies Beer-Lambert to the fixed OD_total column. sphere2J's recurrence mu' = sqrt(1 - (R/R')^2 (1 - mu^2)) conserves the impact parameter R*sin(theta), so each fine level's direction cosine is the closed form mu_i here, and the twilight branch's early exit is the tangent layer b_imp < R_i. Checked against upstream's iterative calc_direct_flux on 4309 (P, cosSZA) points x 18 bands, every level and twilight included: worst relative difference 4.4e-11. Diffuse field: upstream's diffuse_flux_data.bson table with the same bilinear, flat-extrapolated lookup upstream uses.\n\nWhat is transcribed verbatim from upstream: cos_solar_zenith_angle (its own declination/equation-of-time formula, NOT lib/solar.esm's NOAA Spencer series, including upstream's abs(lat) - southern-hemisphere points get the northern-hemisphere seasonal declination term - and its mod(year, 4) leap rule); solar_flux_factor; every cross section and quantum yield the 13 rates use, knot for knot as the reference evaluates them (bins whose two temperature rows agree within Float32 isapprox are the constant upstream returns); ActAld's cross section on the pressure axis (P/100, hPa) as upstream's _FJX_PRESSURE_AXIS_J requires; adjust_j_o31D. The J set is upstream's `mech = :superfast` list (the rates SuperFast and Pollu consume); upstream FastJX also exposes ~95 GEOS-Chem channels that are not carried here. datetime.* decomposes t_utc with integer seconds, where Julia's unix2datetime rounds to the millisecond; the two differ only within 0.5 ms of a second boundary.\n\nTolerance rationale (rel = 1e-06): the expected values are the reference package's own Julia functions at the test inputs. The ESM evaluates the same arithmetic in Float64 from the same constants; what remains is roundoff (upstream forms the cross-section slope (x2 - x1) in Float32) and the closed-form-vs-iterative direct beam (<= 4.4e-11); measured, no assertion here is off by more than 3.0e-8 relative, and the worst over 17 outputs at 300 random (t, lat, long, T, P, H2O) points is 3.3e-8. abs = 1e-22 floors rates that are exactly 0 at night.\n\nTest coverage: each scenario asserts cosSZA, solf, five band fluxes (295-574 nm) and j_NO2, with j_o32OH at local noon; the 13 J-rates, cross sections and adjust_j_o31D, which this file shares with fastjx_interp_troposphere.esm, are asserted in full there. The split is for the Julia runner, whose test-time observed reader re-evaluates an observed's whole dependency tree on every read: one J read here walks every band's 146 air-mass factors and costs ~14 s, so the full set would take ~30 min."
      },
      "variables": {
        "T": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "K",
          "default": 298.0,
          "description": "Air temperature (K). Selects the temperature-dependent cross sections and O(1D) quantum yields and enters adjust_j_o31D. Upstream default 298 K."
        },
        "P": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "Pa",
          "default": 101325.0,
          "description": "Air pressure (Pa). Selects the actinic-flux level and enters adjust_j_o31D; ActAld's cross section is interpolated on P/100 (hPa). Upstream default 101325 Pa."
        },
        "H2O": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "ppb",
          "default": 450.0,
          "description": "Water-vapour mixing ratio (ppb); enters adjust_j_o31D only. Upstream default 450 ppb (the reference couples it from GEOS-FP RH)."
        },
        "lat": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "deg",
          "default": 40.0,
          "description": "Latitude (degrees north). Upstream default 40."
        },
        "long": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "deg",
          "default": -97.0,
          "description": "Longitude (degrees east). Upstream default -97. Named `long` as upstream."
        },
        "t_ref": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "s",
          "default": 1462060800.0,
          "description": "Reference Unix time (s) of t = 0; the absolute time is t + t_ref. Upstream takes it as the constructor argument (DomainInfo's t_ref); default 2016-05-01T00:00:00Z."
        },
        "t_utc": {"type": "unknown", "units": "s", "description": "Absolute UTC time (Unix s) = t + t_ref."},
        "DOY": {"type": "unknown", "units": "1", "description": "UTC day of year (1 = Jan 1)."},
        "utc_hours": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "UTC hour of day, hour + minute/60 + second/3600 (upstream `hours`)."
        },
        "year": {"type": "unknown", "units": "1", "description": "UTC calendar year."},
        "yearday_temp": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "2*pi*(DOY - 1 + (hours - 12)/24), the un-normalised fractional-year angle."
        },
        "gamma": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "Fractional-year angle (rad): yearday_temp/366 when mod(year, 4) == 0, else /365 (upstream's leap rule)."
        },
        "Eot": {"type": "unknown", "units": "1", "description": "Equation of time (minutes)."},
        "timezone": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "floor(long/15): the nominal time-zone offset (hours)."
        },
        "time_offset": {"type": "unknown", "units": "1", "description": "Eot + 4*(long - 15*timezone) (minutes)."},
        "t_local": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "s",
          "description": "Zone-local time (Unix s) = t_utc + timezone*3600."
        },
        "tst": {"type": "unknown", "units": "1", "description": "True solar time (hours)."},
        "AHR": {"type": "unknown", "units": "rad", "description": "Hour angle (rad) = deg2rad(15)*(tst - 12)."},
        "LAT": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "rad",
          "description": "abs(lat) in radians. Upstream takes the absolute value (see notes: southern-hemisphere seasons are not flipped)."
        },
        "DEC": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "rad",
          "description": "Solar declination (rad): asin(sin(deg2rad(-23.44)) * cos(deg2rad(360/365.24*(DOY+10) + 360/pi*0.0167*sin(deg2rad(360/365.24*(DOY-2))))))."
        },
        "cosSZA": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "Cosine of the solar zenith angle (upstream cos_solar_zenith_angle; unclamped)."
        },
        "solf": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "Earth-Sun distance factor on the TOA flux, 1 - 0.034*cos((DOY - 172)*2*pi/365) (upstream solar_flux_factor, after GEOS-Chem SOLAR_JX SOLFX)."
        },
        "lev_index": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "Upstream find_closest_pressure_index(P, P_levels): 1 above the surface edge, else the number of the 73 GEOS 72-layer edges (at Psurf = 101325 Pa) with P_level >= P. The direct beam is evaluated at that edge, so it is piecewise constant in P."
        },
        "fine_index": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "2*lev_index - 1: the edge's index on the 147-point fine grid."
        },
        "R_f": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "cm",
          "description": "Radius (cm) of the evaluation edge, Earth radius 6375 km + ZHL[lev_index] (upstream calcRZ2). interp.linear at an integer knot returns the table entry exactly."
        },
        "Rf_u0": {"type": "unknown", "units": "cm", "description": "R_f*cosSZA."},
        "b_imp": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "cm",
          "description": "Impact parameter of the slant solar ray, R_f*sin(SZA). Upstream's sphere2J iterates sqrt(1 - (R_i/R_i+1)^2 (1 - mu^2)), which conserves R*sin(theta) = b_imp, so every level's direction cosine has the closed form used by mu_i."
        },
        "dark": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "1 when the ray is in the Earth's shadow (upstream shadht test, cosSZA < 0 and b_imp < R_1), else 0."
        },
        "mu_1": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "Direction cosine of the solar ray at fine-grid radius R_1 = 6.375000e+08 cm: sqrt(max(0, 1 - (b_imp/R_i)^2)), written to be exact at R_i = R_f (mu = |cosSZA|)."
        },
        "mu_2": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "Direction cosine of the solar ray at fine-grid radius R_2 = 6.375062e+08 cm: sqrt(max(0, 1 - (b_imp/R_i)^2)), written to be exact at R_i = R_f (mu = |cosSZA|)."
        },
